    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186115393,
      "id": "d54a6dbe-42cb-5614-ae0b-611de1719b45",
      "title": "Using the Product Rules for Differentiation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2ece0cfb-deef-50f1-ac37-153df34a75ee",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find $(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{\\prime}$ given that $\\mathbf{f}(t)=⟨e^{2 t + 2},\\,e^{t},\\,e^{- 2 t}⟩$ and $v(t)=e^{2 t}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the scalar function multiple rule:\n\n$$\n(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{\\prime}=v(t)\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t)+v^{\\prime}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{2 t + 2})=2 e^{2 t + 2} \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{t})=e^{t} \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{- 2 t})=- 2 e^{- 2 t} \\\\ \\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t) & =⟨2 e^{2 t + 2},\\,e^{t},\\,- 2 e^{- 2 t}⟩ \\\\ v^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{2 t})=2 e^{2 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the first product-rule term and the second product-rule term, denoted $P_1$ and $P_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_1 & =(e^{2 t})⟨2 e^{2 t + 2},\\,e^{t},\\,- 2 e^{- 2 t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 e^{2 t} e^{2 t + 2},\\,e^{3 t},\\,-2⟩ \\\\ P_2 & =(2 e^{2 t})⟨e^{2 t + 2},\\,e^{t},\\,e^{- 2 t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 e^{2 t} e^{2 t + 2},\\,2 e^{3 t},\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two terms componentwise:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{\\prime} & =P_1+P_2 \\\\  & =⟨4 e^{4 t + 2},\\,3 e^{3 t},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "bd07970f-28d6-59db-9816-72876f34b77c",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
